WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
SPEAKER 1: Man is unlike
anything else in creation.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:07.590
He sits there with these divine
qualities and simultaneously

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.690
these very earthly qualities.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.469
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.300
We're told in
Genesis, chapter 1:26

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.310
that God has made
man in his image

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.950
and by his likeness
has he made him.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.140
And this is a really
important distinction,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.290
because man is unlike
anything else in creation.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:33.720
He sits there with these divine
qualities and simultaneously

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.060
these very earthly qualities.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.890
And it's the tension
between the two,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.720
the tension between the earthly
and the divine, that comes to--

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.930
it comes to define
man's interaction

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.050
with other humans and the
divine throughout the book.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.500
In Genesis, chapter 2,
verse 5, "On the day

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.300
the Lord God made
Earth and heavens,

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.180
no shrub of the field being
yet on the Earth and no plant

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.120
of the field yet sprouted,
for the Lord God had not

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.800
caused rain to
fall on the Earth,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.050
and there was no human
to till the soil,

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.420
and wetness would
well from the Earth

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.520
to water all the
surface of the soil.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.850
Then God fashioned the
human, humus from soil,

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.550
and blew into his nostrils
the breath of life,

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.670
and the human became
a living creature."

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.130
This is Robert Alter's
translation of Genesis,

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.840
and he's trying to--

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.660
he's trying to depict
for us in English

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:29.010
the wordplay that's
there in Hebrew.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.680
"Adam" just means
"the human being."

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.810
And the human being,
the wordplay there,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.920
it was taken from "Adama,"
which means "from the soil."

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.620
God's taking this human
being from the soil.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:45.000
But note, he is into the
human, once God blows

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.560
into his nostrils.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.790
You see the breath of God.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.970
You see something of the divine
coming down to the human.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.260
It's really a thing of beauty.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.110
You see this kind of
majestic creature,

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.760
unlike the other ones thus far.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.060
And I think the most
startling statement

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.310
in the entirety of
these opening chapters,

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.040
of the first three
chapters of Genesis

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.050
comes now here in verse 18.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.270
"And the Lord God
said, 'It is not

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.039
good for the human to be alone.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:14.130
I shall make him a sustainer
beside him.'" For the first

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:19.320
time, if you go back to Genesis
chapter 1, God repeatedly says,

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.800
"And it was good," "and it
was good," "and it was good."

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.650
Every act of creation is good.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.870
All things are good
in this narrative.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.380
But now we are going to get
a key insight with regards

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.380
to the human and really
what it means to be a human.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.170
Because for the first
time, we are told

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.910
that something is not good.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.430
And it is not good
that man is alone.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:46.710
This comes to help us understand
that we aren't these autonomous

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:50.190
beings, answerable to no one.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.410
We would like to say we're
only answerable to God.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.660
But even here, we
understand there

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.080
is something about what it
means to be the human here,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.110
that being in isolation
is wholly unacceptable.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:03.460
And so what does God do?

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.180
He starts fashioning animals.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.770
"The Lord fashioned
from the soil

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.440
each beast of the field and
each fowl of the heavens

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.960
and brought each to the human
to see what he would call it.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.450
Whatever the human
called a living creature,

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.430
that was its name."

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.850
But of course, we're
told after all of this,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:26.160
there was no sustainer found
for the human in these animals.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.640
Verse 21, "And the Lord God cast
a deep slumber on the human,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:30.570
and he slept.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.390
And He took one of his ribs
and closed over the flesh

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:34.410
where it had been.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.900
And the Lord God built the rib
he had taken from the human

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.330
into a woman, and He
brought her to the human.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.540
And the human said,
'This one at last,

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.360
bone of my bones and
flesh of my flesh,

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.260
this one shall be called Woman,
for from man was this one

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.990
taken.'"

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:54.500
It's this beautiful moment,
because what we see here

00:03:54.500 --> 00:04:00.980
with regards to humankind's
notion of personhood

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.860
is that it's a
corporate personhood,

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:08.090
is that to understand what
it means to be a human person

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:11.330
isn't simply to be a unique
individual surrounded

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.700
by all of these other
unique individuals made

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:14.510
in the same way.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:18.380
But rather, we are in
a sense incomplete.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.930
We can't even name who we are.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:23.660
We have no identity
if we are not

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.780
in a relationship to
another human person.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:31.640
In fact, look at the poem
that Adam offers to us.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.610
"This one at last, bone of my
bones and flesh of my flesh."

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:37.250
Notice he names
this one precisely

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.890
in the way in which he's
going to name the animals.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:43.640
This one shall be called Woman--
the Hebrew, isha, also a word

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.900
that's used for wife.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.300
This one shall be
called woman, isha.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.590
And now notice what he does.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:51.560
For from man, ish,
was this one taken.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.870
It's a beautiful moment,
because now what you see

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.020
is in the act of naming this
other human being across

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:00.200
from him, only then can
he actually name himself

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:01.910
as a separate identity.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.920
Because it's really not
a separate identity.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.080
They're bound to one another.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.450
The very notion
of personhood here

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.600
is found here in
this relationship.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.210
From isha, only
once that's named

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.170
can I actually now have an
understanding of myself.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:22.910
And so this begins to stake
out what the human-to-the-human

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.200
relationship is.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:30.050
And then what we will see,
come chapter 3, is a fall,

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:33.620
is a disruption, is a rupture
of this human-to-human

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:34.250
relationship.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.950
And it turns out the rupture
in this human-to-human

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:41.120
relationship, where she is
to be a sustainer to him, he

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:44.720
is to find his own
personhood there in isha,

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.580
what we'll find
here is it's also

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.810
a disruption in the divine
and human relationship.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:52.260
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you for watching.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.230
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlight series

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.140
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:05:57.140 --> 00:05:58.340
online courses.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:02.470
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

